<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="intro-comp">
    <title>Setting the Stage (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="10in">
            <title>Why Math 271?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Preparation for Physical Chemistry, which involves describing <em>quantum particles</em>. 
                        The need for strong preparation in mathematics is apparent by reading the postulates of quantum mechanics 
                        (from <em>Physical Chemistry: A Molecular Approach</em>, Ch. 4):
                        <dl>
                            <li>
                                <title>Postulate 1</title>
                                <p>
                                    The state of a quantum-mechanical system is completely specified by a function <m>\psi(x)</m> 
                                    that depends upon the coordinate of the particle. All possible information about the system
                                    can be derived from <m>\psi(x)</m>. This function, called the wave function or the state function,
                                    has the important property that <m>\overline{\psi(x)}\psi(x)dx</m> is the probability that the particle
                                    lies in the interval <m>dx</m>, located at the position <m>x</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <title>Postulate 2</title>
                                <p>
                                    To every observable in classical mechanics there corresponds a linear, Hermitian operator in quantum 
                                    mechanics.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <title>Postulate 3</title>
                                <p>
                                    In any measurement of the observable associated with the operator <m>\hat{A}</m>, the only values 
                                    that will every be observed are the eigenvalues <m>a_n</m>, which satisfy the eigenvalue equation
                                    <md>
                                        \hat{A}\psi_n = a_n\psi_n
                                    </md>.
                                    
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <title>Postulate 4</title>
                                <p>
                                    If a system is in a state described by a normalized wave function <m>\psi</m>, then the average value
                                    of the observable corresponding to <m>\hat{A}</m> is given by
                                    <md>
                                        \langle a \rangle = \int_{\mathrm{all space}} \overline{\psi}\hat{A}\psi dx
                                    </md>.
                                    
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <title>Postulate 5</title>
                                <p>
                                    The wave function, or state function, of a system evolves in time according to the time-dependent 
                                    Schrödinger equation
                                    <md>
                                        \hat{H}\psi(x,t)= i \overline{h}\frac{\partial \psi}{\partial t}
                                    </md>.
                                    There's lots of foundational mathematics baked into this equation:
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </dl>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Complex numbers and functions
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    (Multivariable) functions
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    (Linear) Operators
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Matrices and Vectors (Linear Algebra)
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    (Partial) derivatives
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    (Partial) differential equations
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="2in" >
            <title>How?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   We'll explore these important topics (and more!) in four main units:
                </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Functions
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Multivariable Calculus
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Linear Algebra
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                   Differential Equations
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                    <p>            
                        There are full courses on each of these topics, so our goals will include building intuition, 
                        understanding notation, and working with/manipulating/computing with objects in meaningful ways.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        We'll achieve our goals through lectures/visuals (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday) and lots of 
                        hands-on practice and assessment (Friday).
                    </p>
                    
                </solution>
            </exercise>


